---
import LegalLayout from "@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/consts";
---

<LegalLayout
	title="Privacy Policy"
	description="Bramble is a local-first password manager. It stores everything encrypted on your device. Its network use is limited to optional, off-by-default features you control (a password breach check, peer-to-peer sync between your own devices, a mailbox that holds encrypted changes for devices that are never online together, encrypted backups to a storage provider you choose, and email aliases from a provider you already use), plus an update check in the desktop and mobile apps that you can turn off."
	path="/privacy"
	meta="Effective date: 27 November 2026"
>
	<p class="lead">
		<strong>The short version:</strong> Bramble is a local-first password manager, available as a
		browser extension, as mobile apps, and as a desktop app. It does not collect, sell, or share any
		of your data. There is no Bramble server that holds your vault, and no account unless you choose
		Bramble's paid mailbox, whose account is just a number. Everything stays on your device,
		encrypted. Bramble makes network connections only for a few optional features that are off by
		default and that you turn on yourself: a password breach check, peer-to-peer sync between your
		own devices, a mailbox that holds encrypted changes for devices that are never online together,
		encrypted backups to a storage provider you choose, and email aliases from a provider you
		already use. The one exception is a check for updates in the desktop
		and mobile apps; you can turn that off. None of these ever exposes your passwords or your
		vault in a form that Bramble, a provider, or anyone else could read. The website, bramble.sh,
		has no analytics or cookies, and keeps an email address only if you subscribe to the
		newsletter. Details below.
	</p>

	<h2>What data Bramble handles</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble stores the data you choose to save in it, such as login usernames and passwords,
		passkeys, payment cards, secure notes, SSH keys, two-factor (TOTP) seeds, and files you attach
		to entries, together with your own app preferences. This data is created and used only to provide the password-manager
		features you
		asked for (saving, searching, and autofilling your credentials).
	</p>

	<h2>Where it is stored</h2>
	<p>
		Your vault is stored only on your devices. In the browser extension that means your browser's
		local extension storage and/or a vault file you pick on your own computer. In the mobile apps
		it means the app's private on-device storage, with a small number of secrets (the key that
		protects your vault) held in the OS keystore. In the desktop app it means ordinary files in the
		app's data folder on your computer; this device's sync identity and the credentials for any
		cloud backups you set up are kept in your operating system's credential store where one is
		available (the macOS Keychain, the Windows Credential Manager, or the Secret Service or kernel
		keyring on Linux), and never the key to your vault. Bramble has no vault server, so your data is not
		stored off your devices unless you turn on cloud backups (below), which keep an encrypted copy
		with a storage provider you choose. The optional mailbox (below) holds encrypted changes only
		until your devices collect them, never your vault.
	</p>

	<h2>How it is protected</h2>
	<p>
		Your vault is encrypted. The key that protects it is derived from your master password using
		Argon2id, and individual entries are encrypted with AES-256-GCM. Encryption and decryption
		happen locally on your device. Your master password and the keys derived from it are never
		stored in plaintext and never leave your device.
	</p>

	<h2>What Bramble does not do</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>No data collection, tracking, analytics, telemetry, or advertising.</li>
		<li>
			No accounts, sign-up, or login to any service, except the optional Bramble Mailbox account,
			which is a number with no email, name, or password (see "The mailbox" below).
		</li>
		<li>
			No Bramble vault server or cloud. Bramble runs no server that stores your vault. If you turn
			on backups, only an encrypted copy goes to a storage provider you choose and control (see
			"Cloud backups" below); the ciphertext is all that ever leaves your device.
		</li>
		<li>No selling, renting, or sharing of your data with anyone.</li>
		<li>No use of your data for any purpose other than the features you use.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Network connections, in full</h2>
	<p>
		The browser extension works entirely offline by default, and its updates come through the store
		you installed it from. There are five features that make network connections, all optional and
		all off until you turn them on: the password breach check, device sync, the mailbox, cloud
		backups, and email aliases. The desktop and mobile apps add one more, on by default: a check for updates. They are
		described next. Bramble makes no other network requests.
	</p>

	<h2>Update checks (desktop and mobile apps)</h2>
	<p>
		The desktop app is installed directly rather than from a store, so it looks for new versions
		itself. A few seconds after it opens, it downloads a small file from bramble.sh that names the
		latest version. You can turn this off in Settings → About, under Updates, with "Check for updates when
		Bramble opens"; you can still check by hand from the same place, or from the app menu.
	</p>
	<p>
		The mobile apps do the same at most once a day, so they can tell you when a newer version is
		out. The iPhone app's file names the version live on the App Store, which bramble.sh reads from
		Apple, so your phone does not contact Apple for it. The same Settings switch turns it off.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>What is sent.</strong> An ordinary web request for that file, the same for everyone.
			Nothing from your vault, your settings, or your device is included. Like any web request it
			shows your IP address to the server; bramble.sh is hosted by Cloudflare.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Nothing installs without you.</strong> If a newer version exists, Bramble asks before
			doing anything. If you agree, the desktop app downloads the update from Bramble's releases on
			GitHub and checks it against a signature built into the app before applying it, so a tampered
			download is refused. On iPhone, Bramble opens its App Store page. On Android, it opens the
			update's download from GitHub in your browser, and Android installs it only if it is signed
			with the same key as the Bramble you have.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Not every install checks.</strong> On Linux, copies installed from a package (such as
			the .deb or .rpm, or through Nix) never check, because your package manager updates them; the
			AppImage does check.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Password breach check (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble can tell you whether one of your saved passwords has appeared in a known data breach.
		This feature is off by default; you turn it on in Settings.
	</p>
	<p>
		When it is on, Bramble checks a password using the Have I Been Pwned "Pwned Passwords" service
		with a privacy-preserving method called k-anonymity: it computes a SHA-1 hash of the password
		and sends only the first five characters of that hash to its API (api.pwnedpasswords.com). The
		service returns a list of possible matches and Bramble compares them on your device. Your
		password, and even its full hash, never leave your device, and Have I Been Pwned cannot tell
		which password you checked. Bramble also asks the service to pad its response so the amount of
		data returned reveals nothing about your query.
	</p>
	<p>
		Have I Been Pwned is operated by a third party under its own privacy policy:{" "}
		<a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com/Privacy">haveibeenpwned.com/Privacy</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Syncing across your own devices (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble can sync one vault across your own devices (for example between two browsers, or between
		a browser and a phone). This is optional and off until you set it up by linking a device, and
		it is strictly personal: only your own devices, only your own vault. There is no shared network,
		and no server ever stores your vault or can read it.
	</p>
	<p>How it works, and what each part can see:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>The vault transfer is device-to-device.</strong> Your devices connect directly to
			each other wherever the network allows it, and exchange the vault over that encrypted
			connection. The vault stays end-to-end encrypted under a key that only your devices hold.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>A signaling relay only helps your devices find each other.</strong> To set up the
			direct connection, your devices exchange brief connection details through a relay (a WebSocket
			server). By default this is a relay we run, but you can point Bramble at your own relay or at
			any public relay that speaks the same protocol. The relay never receives your vault, your
			passwords, your master password, or any key that could decrypt them.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The relay sees only opaque data and basic metadata.</strong> The signaling messages
			it passes along are themselves encrypted with a key it does not have, and the "room" your
			devices meet in is addressed by an unguessable value derived from that key, so the relay
			cannot tie it to you. The most it can observe is that two network addresses connected, and
			when. The direct channel between your devices is mutually authenticated, so a dishonest relay
			cannot read, alter, or impersonate either device; it can at most refuse to help, in which case
			you can link devices by scanning a QR code instead.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>When a direct connection is not possible, it is relayed.</strong> Two devices on
			different networks (a phone on mobile data and a laptop at home, say) often cannot reach each
			other directly. Then the connection runs through a TURN relay server, Cloudflare's by default,
			which the signaling relay arranges. The relayed traffic is the same end-to-end encrypted
			connection, which the TURN server cannot read; it sees your network addresses and how much data
			passes. File attachments can travel this way too, still encrypted, so for those it learns about
			how large each one is, never its name or contents. You can point Bramble at your own TURN service in the same Advanced settings as the
			relay. To keep that free service from being abused, the signaling relay limits how often an
			address may ask for TURN access, and for that keeps a salted hash of the address that asked
			(never the address itself) for at most a day.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>The mailbox (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		Devices that are never online at the same time can sync through a mailbox: a server that holds
		your encrypted changes until each of your devices has collected them, and then deletes them. It
		is off until you turn it on, and it only works alongside device sync. You can use Bramble's
		mailbox, which is paid, or run the open-source mailbox server yourself, in which case none of
		what follows involves us.
	</p>
	<p>What Bramble's mailbox holds, and what it can see:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Encrypted changes, never your vault in readable form.</strong> Each change, and each
			file attachment that travels with one, is encrypted on your device under a key only your
			devices hold. The mailbox cannot read them, and never receives your master password or any key
			that could decrypt them.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Your devices, by key only.</strong> The mailbox knows each of your devices by the
			public key it signs its requests with, and keeps the day each one last checked in. For each
			change waiting for delivery it keeps which device sent it, its size, and when. It has no device
			names and cannot tell which vault the changes belong to.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Delivered means deleted.</strong> A change is deleted as soon as every device it was
			meant for has collected it, and after 365 days at the most. A mailbox none of whose devices
			has checked in for 365 days is deleted with everything in it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Where it runs.</strong> On Cloudflare Workers, with its data stored in Cloudflare's EU
			jurisdiction. Cloudflare sees the network address each request comes from, as any host does.
			Bramble keeps no request logs: when something fails, it records the error, never the request,
			a network address, or an account.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Rate limits.</strong> To stop account numbers from being guessed, requests that name
			an account are limited per network address. The limiter is given a hash of the address keyed
			with a secret, never the address itself.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>The Bramble Mailbox account and paying for it:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>The account is a number.</strong> An account is a random 25-digit number, with no
			email, name, or password. The mailbox stores a keyed hash of the number, never the number
			itself, together with when the account was opened, its paid time, whether it is paused over
			a disputed payment, and each time that was added to it (when, and how many days). It keeps
			these until you delete the account.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Where the number is kept.</strong> In the apps, it is kept in your vault's settings,
			encrypted with the rest of your vault, and synced to your devices. On bramble.sh it is kept
			only in that browser tab, for the trip to the payment page and back. It is sent only in the
			body of a request, or in the part of a link that browsers never send to a server, so it never
			appears in a web address a server sees, or in a cookie.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>No CAPTCHA.</strong> Opening an account or starting a payment asks your browser to
			solve a small puzzle, so no third party is involved. To limit abuse, those requests are also
			rate-limited using a hash of your network address keyed with a secret and the day; neither
			the address nor the hash is stored.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Card payments go through Stripe.</strong> You pay on Stripe's checkout page. Stripe
			collects your card or wallet details, your email address, and the billing address details it
			needs for the card and for tax, and handles them under its own privacy policy. Stripe is never
			given your account number: the payment carries a random reference, which Bramble deletes once
			the payment arrives. Bramble never receives your card details, and does not keep your email
			address.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Bitcoin and Monero go through our own payment server.</strong> They are handled by
			BTCPay Server, which we run ourselves on a server we rent in Germany, at pay.bramble.sh, with
			no payment company involved. It asks for no email, name, or address. For tax, the account page
			asks which country you live in, and your province in Canada, and guesses nothing from your
			connection. Bramble sends only those and the amount to Stripe to work out the tax, and reports
			a taxed sale to Stripe for its tax records; nothing else about you reaches Stripe. Its invoice
			holds the
			amount, the coin, a payment address made for that invoice, the transaction that paid it, and
			the same random reference a card payment carries, never your account number. Its web server
			keeps no log of visitors' network addresses. The server can see payments arrive but cannot spend them: the keys that
			spend are not on it. To convert prices, it fetches exchange rates on its own, so the rate
			provider never sees you. On the Bitcoin blockchain, a payment's amount and addresses are
			public to anyone, as with any Bitcoin payment; Monero hides them. Crypto payments can't be
			refunded, so no return address is ever asked for.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>What Bramble keeps about a payment.</strong> To handle refunds and disputes, a record
			of each purchase with its account (the days bought, the amount, tax, currency, and country, and
			any refund or dispute), with a card payment's identifier encrypted for the 14-day refund window
			and a keyed hash of the payment's identifier, so a repeat or a dispute can find its account.
			These are deleted after 120 days, after which nothing on our side connects a payment to an
			account. For our accounts and tax returns, Bramble keeps a record of each sale, refund, and
			lost dispute with its date, country, currency, amount, tax, and how it was paid, and no
			account. These records are stored, like the mailbox's, in Cloudflare's EU jurisdiction. The
			payment server keeps its invoices for the same purpose; once a payment arrives, nothing there
			names an account.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Deleting the account.</strong> Deleting it on bramble.sh removes the account, its
			purchase records, and every mailbox it pays for with everything they hold, straight away.
			Deleting does not refund paid time; a purchase can be refunded within 14 days first.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Cloud backups (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble can keep an encrypted backup of your vault with a storage provider you choose. This is
		optional and off until you set it up, and you can turn it off or remove your backups at any
		time.
	</p>
	<p>What this does and does not expose:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Only ciphertext leaves your device.</strong> A backup is the same
			client-side-encrypted blob as a local export: it can be opened only with your master password.
			Encryption happens on your device before anything is uploaded, so the provider stores opaque
			data and never sees your passwords, your vault, or any key that could decrypt them.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>You pick the destination, and Bramble has no server in the middle.</strong> Bramble
			uploads directly to the storage you configure: Dropbox or Google Drive (which you connect with
			a normal sign-in), or any S3-compatible or WebDAV storage, including services you self-host.
			Bramble runs no backup server of its own and keeps no copy.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The provider is a third party under its own terms.</strong> Whatever storage you
			choose receives the encrypted file and the account or connection details needed to store it,
			and handles them under its own privacy policy. Because the file is encrypted, that is all it
			can see.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Google Drive backups (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		If you connect Google Drive as a backup destination, Bramble asks Google for one permission,
		<code>drive.file</code>. It lets Bramble see and change only the files Bramble creates. Bramble
		cannot see, read, or change anything else in your Google Drive.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>What Bramble does with it.</strong> It creates a folder named "Bramble" in your My
			Drive, uploads your encrypted backups there, lists them, and deletes the oldest once there are
			more than the number you chose to keep. It uses the access for nothing else.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Your Google sign-in stays on your device.</strong> When you connect, Google gives
			Bramble a token that lets it keep backing up. Bramble keeps that token on your device, sealed
			with your vault key, or in the desktop app, in your operating system's credential store. It
			is never sent to Bramble, which has no server to receive it: requests go straight from your
			device to Google.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Nothing is shared.</strong> Bramble does not share data from your Google account with
			anyone, does not use it for advertising, and does not use it to train AI models. Because it
			never leaves your device, no person at Bramble can read it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Turning it off.</strong> Remove the Google Drive destination in Bramble's Settings,
			then remove Bramble's access in your Google Account under{" "}
			<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">Third-party apps &amp; services</a>. Your
			backup files stay in your Drive until you delete them.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Bramble's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will adhere
		to the{" "}
		<a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
			>Google API Services User Data Policy</a
		>, including the Limited Use requirements.
	</p>

	<h2>Email aliases (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble can create a different email address for each site you sign up to, using an alias
		provider you already have an account with. This is optional and off until you set it up, and
		setting it up is what turns it on: until you enter an API key in Settings, Bramble never
		contacts any alias provider and the feature does not appear anywhere in the app.
	</p>
	<p>What this does and does not expose:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Nothing happens without a deliberate click.</strong> An address is only ever created
			when you ask for one, by pressing the alias button in an entry, or the alias row that appears
			on a sign-up form. Bramble never creates one in the background, never on page load, and never
			to see whether it could.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Bramble is not the alias service and never sees your mail.</strong> Your provider is
			the one that creates the address and forwards the mail; Bramble asks it for an address on your
			behalf and puts the answer in the field. Mail never passes through Bramble, and Bramble runs
			no alias service of its own.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>What is sent, and to whom.</strong> The request goes to your provider and nowhere
			else: to Addy.io, SimpleLogin, Fastmail, DuckDuckGo Email Protection or Firefox Relay, or to
			your own server if you self-host Addy.io or SimpleLogin. It carries your API key, the site the
			address is for, and a short note naming Bramble, so the alias is recognisable in your
			provider's own dashboard. Fastmail gets the site but no note, because it asks apps to leave
			that field to you and already shows the name you gave the API key. DuckDuckGo accepts neither
			the site nor the note, so a request to it carries your key and nothing else. Nothing from your
			vault is included.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Your API key stays encrypted on your device.</strong> It is stored under your vault's
			encryption key, like the rest of your vault, and is used only to authenticate these requests.
			Because it is encrypted with the vault, an alias can be created only while the vault is
			unlocked. Removing the provider in Settings deletes the key, and deleting the vault deletes
			it too.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The provider is a third party under its own terms.</strong> Your provider receives the
			requests above and handles them under its own privacy policy, and the aliases you create live
			in your account with it, where you can disable or delete them.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Autofill</h2>
	<p>
		In the browser extension, Bramble's content script runs on web pages and looks at the structure
		of forms on the page (for example, whether a field is a username, password, or one-time-code
		field) so it can offer to fill saved credentials. This happens entirely on your device. Bramble
		does not read, collect, or transmit the content of the pages you visit, and it only fills a
		credential when you explicitly choose one.
	</p>
	<p>
		On mobile, autofill uses the operating system's own AutoFill framework. When you pick Bramble to
		fill a login, the system hands Bramble the app or website you are filling so it can find a
		matching entry. Bramble's autofill runs in a separate, locked component that reads your vault
		only after you authenticate (with biometrics, your device passcode, or your master password) and
		then decrypt it; nothing in your vault is readable before that.
	</p>

	<h2>Passkeys and security keys</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble can act as a passkey provider (and, in the browser extension, a security-key provider)
		for the sites and apps you sign in to. When a site asks you to create or use a passkey, your
		browser or device can offer Bramble: the passkey's private key is generated and kept in your
		encrypted vault, stored as an ordinary vault entry, and never leaves it. Bramble only signs the
		challenge the site sends, and only after you approve. In Chromium browsers this uses the
		browser's Web Authentication proxy; on mobile it uses the operating system's passkey framework.
		No passkey material is ever sent to Bramble.
	</p>

	<h2>On mobile: unlocking, key storage, and keyboard suggestions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Biometric unlock.</strong> If you turn it on, Bramble caches your vault key behind a
			hardware-backed biometric gate provided by the operating system (the iOS Keychain and Secure
			Enclave, or the Android Keystore). The key is released only after a successful biometric or
			device-passcode check, and the system invalidates it if your enrolled biometrics change. This
			is a convenience built on the operating system's own protection; it stays on your device.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Shared storage with the autofill component.</strong> So that AutoFill can work, its
			autofill component reads your logins from encrypted, app-private storage on the device. That
			storage is protected with your vault key and is not readable without unlocking, and nothing in
			it ever leaves the device.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Keyboard suggestions (iOS, off by default).</strong> On iOS you can optionally let
			Bramble show matching logins as suggestions above the keyboard. Turning this on provides your
			saved usernames and the websites they belong to (but never your passwords) to iOS, so the
			system can show those suggestions even before you unlock Bramble. It is off by default
			precisely because it makes those usernames visible to the operating system before
			authentication; you can turn it off at any time.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Camera.</strong> Bramble can scan QR codes (to link a device for sync, or to add a
			TOTP secret) using your camera. The scanning happens on your device and the camera images are
			not stored or transmitted.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>On the desktop: the browser link, credentials, and starting at login</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Linking to your browser.</strong> The desktop app can link to the Bramble extension in
			browsers on the same computer, so its quick-access panel can fill the page in front of you.
			The two talk over a channel the browser provides between itself and programs on the same
			computer ("native messaging"), encrypted and authenticated by a pairing you confirm, and
			nothing about it goes over the network. To make that possible, the desktop app registers a
			small helper with the browsers installed on your computer: a small file for each browser, or on
			Windows one file plus a registry entry for each browser.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Credentials.</strong> As above, the keys that identify this device to your others for
			sync, and the credentials for any cloud backups you set up, are kept in your operating
			system's credential store. Your vault key never is.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Starting at login (off by default).</strong> If you turn it on, the app starts when you
			sign in to your computer, so scheduled backups run without you opening it.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Browser extension permissions, in plain language</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Access to websites: to detect login fields and offer autofill.</li>
		<li>Storage: to keep your encrypted vault and preferences on your device.</li>
		<li>Clipboard: to copy a password and clear it after a timeout.</li>
		<li>
			Alarms and idle: to lock the vault automatically after inactivity or when your screen locks.
		</li>
		<li>Offscreen: to run the encryption module.</li>
		<li>
			Web authentication: to let you use Bramble as a passkey or security-key provider for websites
			(Chromium browsers).
		</li>
		<li>
			Identity: to run the provider's sign-in screen when you connect a cloud-backup account such as
			Dropbox or Google Drive.
		</li>
		<li>
			Native messaging (optional, Chromium browsers): to talk to the Bramble desktop app on the same
			computer. Your browser asks you for it only when you link the extension to the desktop app.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Clipboard</h2>
	<p>
		When you copy a password, Bramble writes it to your clipboard and, after a short timeout, clears
		the clipboard to reduce the chance of a secret lingering there. This is done locally and the
		copied value is never sent anywhere.
	</p>

	<h2>Importing other password files</h2>
	<p>
		If you import a vault from another manager (for example a KeePass .kdbx file), Bramble reads that
		file locally to add its entries to your vault. The file and its contents are processed only on
		your device and are not transmitted anywhere.
	</p>

	<h2>Recovery and lost access</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble has no copy of your master password or the keys derived from it, so it cannot recover
		your vault or reset your password for you. If you set up a recovery code or a security key, you
		can use those to regain access. If you lose your master password and every recovery method you
		set up, your vault cannot be decrypted by you, by us, or by anyone. That is the trade-off for
		there being no backdoor.
	</p>

	<h2>This website (bramble.sh)</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>No analytics or cookies.</strong> bramble.sh runs no analytics or tracking scripts and
			sets no cookies. It remembers your light or dark theme in your browser's own storage, and
			sends it nowhere.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Hosting.</strong> Cloudflare serves the site. Like any host, it sees your network
			address, and it keeps the standard traffic statistics it keeps for every site it serves.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The newsletter (optional).</strong> If you subscribe, your email address goes to
			Buttondown, which keeps the list and sends the newsletter under its own privacy policy:{" "}
			<a href="https://buttondown.com/legal/privacy">buttondown.com/legal/privacy</a>. Subscribing
			opens a Buttondown page to confirm your address, and every issue has a link to unsubscribe.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Data retention and deletion</h2>
	<p>
		Because your data lives only on your devices, you are in control of it. Removing the extension
		or the app, deleting your vault file, and clearing the app's or extension's storage removes your
		data. For the desktop app, that means deleting its data folder too; the Windows uninstaller
		offers to do that for you, along with the credentials it stored in the Windows Credential
		Manager. Apart from Bramble's mailbox and the newsletter, Bramble retains nothing on your behalf
		because it holds nothing about you anywhere else. If you use the mailbox, see "The mailbox"
		above for what it keeps, for how long, and how deleting your account removes it. If you
		subscribed to the newsletter, unsubscribing from any issue takes you off the list. If you turned on cloud backups,
		delete the encrypted backup files from your storage provider too, since those live in your
		account there, not with Bramble.
	</p>

	<h2>Children</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble is a general-purpose tool, not directed at children, and collects no information from
		anyone beyond what the optional mailbox and newsletter need, described above.
	</p>

